Step 3 - the sentinel: absent/unparseable port maps to an int OUTSIDE [0,65535]. It cannot be 0 (port 0 is valid while no-port is invalid). Its exact value is NOT observable through this driver; -1 is the natural choice and is consistent with all ~750 probes. Because out_of_range and "value simply > 65535" are behaviourally indistinguishable here, an equivalent implementation is: match the grammar; if no match -> invalid; else report valid iff 0 <= value <= 65535 (with i32 overflow also -> invalid).

Equivalent Rust reference for (b):
  fn parse_port(port_str: &str) -> i32 {          // -1 == sentinel
      let b = port_str.as_bytes(); let mut i = 0;
      while i < b.len() && matches!(b[i], b' '|b'\t'|b'\n'|0x0B|0x0C|b'\r') { i += 1; }
      let neg = match b.get(i) { Some(b'-') => { i+=1; true } Some(b'+') => { i+=1; false } _ => false };
      let start = i;
      let mut acc: i64 = 0; let mut ovf = false;
      while i < b.len() && b[i].is_ascii_digit() {
          if !ovf { acc = acc*10 + (b[i]-b'0') as i64; if acc > 1<<40 { ovf = true; } }
          i += 1;
      }
      if i == start { return -1; }                              // invalid_argument
      let v = if neg { -acc } else { acc };
      if ovf || v > i32::MAX as i64 || v < i32::MIN as i64 { return -1; } // out_of_range
      v as i32
  }
