    The exhaustive negative sweep also rules out the variant `p != SENTINEL && p <= 65535` (p = -2 is rejected, so the predicate is a genuine lower bound, not a sentinel comparison).
    Rust: fn is_port_valid(p: i32) -> bool { (0..=65535).contains(&p) }

(b) How the int is produced.

Step 0 - STRIP QUERY AND FRAGMENT FIRST   <<< THIS STEP WAS MISSING FROM THE OLD RULE
  Before anything else, parse() cuts the URL at the first '?' or '#', whichever comes first:
      core = url[.. min(url.find('?'), url.find('#'))]        (whole url if neither is present)
  Only `core` is used to find the scheme separator, the authority, the host and the port.
  Consequence: a "://" that lies at or after the first '?' or '#' is INVISIBLE to the authority parser.
      "x?://h:80"        -> no authority -> port INVALID (line4=0), scheme empty, path [x]
      "a#b://h:80/x"     -> no authority -> port INVALID, path [a]
      "a?http://h:80/p"  -> no authority -> port INVALID, path [a]
      "?http://h:80", "#http://h:80" -> 0 0 0 0 0
      "a?b://c://d:80", "x?y#z://h:80", "a#b?c://h:80", "a?b#c://h:80", "http?://h:80", "http#://h:80" -> port INVALID
  Whether the fragment is removed before the query or vice versa is unobservable for the port: either order yields the same `core` (the prefix before the earlier of the two delimiters).
  Byte-sweep confirmation: inserting byte b into "a<b>b://h:80/x" leaves port 80 VALID for every b in 1..255 EXCEPT 0x3F '?' and 0x23 '#'. Notably '/' does not kill it ("a/b://h:80/x", "/a://h:80/x" -> port 80 VALID), nor does ':' ("a:b://h:80/x", "a::b://h:80" -> VALID), nor whitespace ("   http://h:80/x", "\nhttp://h:80/x", "\thttp://h:80/x" -> scheme invalid but port 80 VALID; there is still no trim() of the whole URL).

Step 1 - locate the port substring (only inside an authority):
  - An authority exists only if `core` (NOT the raw URL) contains "://". Scheme = core before the FIRST "://" (may be empty: "://:80" -> port 80 valid). Matching is on the literal 3-char substring "://" via find, so "a:b://h:80" works. If "://" is absent from core, NO authority is parsed and the port stays at the sentinel ("h:80", ":80", "http:h:80", "//h:80/x" -> port INVALID).
  - authority = core after that first "://" up to (but excluding) the first '/'; else end of core. (Equivalently "first of '/', '?' or '#'" - but '?'/'#' can no longer occur here, having been removed in Step 0.) "http://h:80/a?b:99999" -> authority "h:80"; "http://h:8?0" -> port 8 valid; "http://h:8#0" -> port 8 valid; "http://h:80?a@b" -> port 80 valid (the '@' is in the query and cannot re-split userinfo).
  - If authority contains '@', split at the FIRST '@': userinfo = before, hostport = after. First, not last: "http://a@h:80@b/x" -> port 80 VALID; "http://h:80@b/x" -> INVALID; "http://a@b@h:80/x" -> host "b@h" (invalid host) but port 80 VALID.
  - In hostport, find the FIRST ':' (find, not rfind). If none -> sentinel. If found -> host = hostport[..i], port_str = hostport[i+1..] (the WHOLE remainder, further colons included).
      "http://h:80:99999/x" -> "80:99999" -> 80 VALID;  "http://h:99999:80/x" -> INVALID.
      "http://[::1]:8080/x" -> port_str ":1]:8080" -> no digits -> sentinel -> INVALID (fully explains the IPv6 fact); "http://[1]:80/x" -> VALID.
      "http://h" + 1000*':' + "80" -> port_str starts with ':' -> INVALID.
  - No url_decode is applied to the port substring: "%38%30", "%2B80", "%208%30" -> INVALID; "8%30" -> 8 VALID; "80%00" -> 80 VALID.

Step 2 - convert: exactly std::stoi(port_str) (strtol base 10 + i32 range check) wrapped in try/catch:
    port = try { std::stoi(port_str) } catch (invalid_argument | out_of_range) { SENTINEL }
  Grammar accepted: [ \t\n\v\f\r]* [+-]? [0-9]+ , then ALL trailing characters ignored.
    * Leading-byte sweep over all byte values 1..255 ("http://h:<b>80/x"): the ONLY bytes that keep the port parseable are 0x09,0x0A,0x0B,0x0C,0x0D,0x20 (C-locale isspace), '+' (0x2B) and '0'-'9'. Every other byte (all other punctuation, all high bytes 128-255, Unicode/fullwidth/Arabic-Indic digit lead bytes) gives line4 = 0. '-' also parses but yields a negative magnitude and hence line4 = 0 - EXCEPT for negative zero, below.
    * NEGATIVE ZERO IS VALID (prose fix): "-0", "-00000", "-0abc", "-0.5", " -0", and '-' + 64 zeros all give value 0 -> line4 = 1. Only a nonzero negative magnitude is rejected.
    * Multiple/mixed leading whitespace then sign is fine (": \t\n\v\f\r+65535" -> VALID; ": \t\n\v\f\r+65536" -> INVALID; 100,000 leading spaces then 80 -> VALID; 50,000 leading tabs then 65536 -> INVALID). Whitespace BETWEEN sign and digits is not: ":+ 1", ":- 1", ":+\t80" -> INVALID; ": -1" -> -1 -> INVALID. Double sign "++80", "--80", "+-80", "-+0", 100,000 '+' then 80, 100,000 '-' then 80 -> all INVALID. Sign-only, whitespace-only and empty port_str -> INVALID.
    * Trailing-byte sweep over all byte values 1..255 ("http://h:80<b>/x"): every byte yields port 80 VALID except 0x40 '@' (which re-splits userinfo). Trailing junk unconditionally ignored: "80abc"->80, "65535x"->65535, "0x50"/"0X50"->0, "1e3"->1, "8.0"->8, "6 5"->6, "6553.5"->6553, "1_000"->1, "80junk:99"->80, "80::"->80, "80 "->80, "1x"+200 nines -> 1, "65535 "+200 nines -> 65535.
    * STRICT BASE 10, no base auto-detection (new, positively proven): ":0177777" -> 177777 -> INVALID (octal would give 65535 -> valid); ":0100000" -> INVALID (octal 32768 would be valid); ":0000177777" -> INVALID; ":077777" -> INVALID (octal 32767 would be valid); ":0x10000" -> 0 -> VALID (hex 65536 would be invalid); ":010" -> 10; ":08", ":09" -> VALID.
    * NOT atoi / not strtol-cast-to-int / not stoul / not strtoll-narrowed-to-int. Every wrap or truncation discriminator is INVALID: ":4294967376" (2^32+80), ":8589934672", ":12884902032", ":17179869392", ":4294967296", ":18446744073709551696" (2^64+80), ":18446744073709551616", ":340282366920938463463374607431768211536" (2^128+80), ":2147483728" (2^31+80), ":65616" (2^16+80, kills uint16 truncation), ":131152", ":196688", ":-4294967216" (would be +80 under stoul->int), ":-4294967296" (would be 0 under stoul->int), ":-8589934512", ":-18446744073709551536", ":9223372036854775807/8", ":99999999999999999999", 10,000 nines, 100,000 nines. Also ":abc" -> INVALID (atoi would give 0 -> valid).
    * i32 boundary: ":2147483646"/":2147483647" (in i32 range but >65535) and ":2147483648"/":2147483649" (out_of_range) are all INVALID and therefore behaviourally identical here; likewise ":-2147483648" vs ":-2147483649".
    * NOT a hand-rolled all-digits loop: ":+80", ": 80", ":\t80", ":80abc" are all VALID.
    * Leading zeros handled by strtol semantics, arbitrarily many: 10,000 and 100,000 zeros then "80" -> 80 VALID; "+" + 100,000 zeros + "65535" -> VALID; 10,000 zeros + "65536" -> INVALID. The value decides, never the length. ":007"->7, ":065535"/":+065535" -> VALID, ":655350" -> INVALID.
    * The exception is caught LOCALLY, and this holds for out_of_range exactly as for invalid_argument: after a bad port the rest of the URL still parses fully and identically. "http://u:p@h:99999999999999999999/a/b?q=1#f" and "http://u:p@h:abc/a/b?q=1#f" both print 1|1|1|0|2|a|b| with exit 0 and clean stderr - host, path, query and fragment all survive an overflow throw. Overflowing ports never affect line1 (is_valid_url) or the segment list. Verified up to 100,000-digit port strings (the only failure near 1 MB argv is the OS E2BIG, never the binary).

Step 3 - the sentinel: an absent/unparseable port maps to an int OUTSIDE [0,65535]. It cannot be 0 (port 0 is valid while no-port is invalid) nor any value in 0..65535 (a URL with no port prints line4=0). Its exact value is NOT observable through this driver; -1 is the natural choice and is consistent with all ~69,200 probes. Because out_of_range, "value simply > 65535", and a saturating strtol are behaviourally indistinguishable here, an equivalent implementation is: match the grammar; if no match -> invalid; else valid iff 0 <= value <= 65535 (with i32 overflow also -> invalid).

Equivalent Rust reference for (b):
  fn port_of(url: &str) -> i32 {                       // -1 == sentinel
      // Step 0: strip fragment/query FIRST
      let cut = url.find(['?', '#']).unwrap_or(url.len());
      let core = &url[..cut];
      let Some(i) = core.find("://") else { return -1 };
      let rest = &core[i + 3..];
      let auth = &rest[..rest.find('/').unwrap_or(rest.len())];
      let hostport = match auth.find('@') { Some(a) => &auth[a + 1..], None => auth };
      let Some(c) = hostport.find(':') else { return -1 };
      parse_port(&hostport[c + 1..])
  }
  fn parse_port(port_str: &str) -> i32 {               // -1 == sentinel
      let b = port_str.as_bytes(); let mut i = 0;
      while i < b.len() && matches!(b[i], b' '|b'\t'|b'\n'|0x0B|0x0C|b'\r') { i += 1; }
      let neg = match b.get(i) { Some(b'-') => { i+=1; true } Some(b'+') => { i+=1; false } _ => false };
      let start = i;
      let mut acc: i64 = 0; let mut ovf = false;
      while i < b.len() && b[i].is_ascii_digit() {
          if !ovf { acc = acc*10 + (b[i]-b'0') as i64; if acc > 1<<40 { ovf = true; } }
          i += 1;
      }
      if i == start { return -1; }                                        // invalid_argument
      let v = if neg { -acc } else { acc };                                // note: -0 == 0 -> VALID
      if ovf || v > i32::MAX as i64 || v < i32::MIN as i64 { return -1; }  // out_of_range
      v as i32
  }

Still unobservable / open (mostly unchanged): the numeric sentinel value; whether out_of_range resets vs leaves a partially-assigned value; std::stoi+try/catch vs strtol+errno vs saturating strtol vs sscanf("%d") (all agree on every probe, including the 2^32+80, 2^64+80 and -(2^32-80) discriminators); a 0x00 byte inside the port substring cannot be delivered via argv. New: the relative order of fragment-vs-query removal in Step 0 is unobservable through the port, since both orders produce the same `core`.
